Negative control for the `G3` declaration law at the `tracks` channel (T4.4).

Moves the client-emails page's playlist OUT of its declaration and into the registry, as a
default every scenario missing a `tracks` channel inherits. Nothing about the
the client-emails page changes — its bar still lists the same tracks from the same seam — so
the mutation is invisible to any assertion that only reads the client-emails page.

What it breaks is the law: the runtime now carries one scenario's knowledge
(its feature, its catalog and its three pinned names, verbatim), and a scenario
declaring NO tracks — `useClientEmail`, the editor — stops rendering Live-only
and inherits a playlist that has nothing to do with it. Generic machinery, per
scenario data; this is the inverse.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds the T4.4 module-lane assertions that (a) no file under `runtime/` names a
scenario's own feature, catalog or pins, and (b) a declaration without `tracks`
resolves without one. No spec on HEAD covers either claim, so this mutant is
green until that spec lands — which is itself the reading: T4.4's oracle does
not exist until the prover writes it.

